NNAAMMEE
       zramctl - set up and control zram devices

SSYYNNOOPPSSIISS
       Get info:
           zzrraammccttll [options]

       Reset zram:
           zzrraammccttll --rr _z_r_a_m_d_e_v...

       Print name of first unused zram device:
           zzrraammccttll --ff

       Set up a zram device:
           zzrraammccttll [--ff | _z_r_a_m_d_e_v] [--ss _s_i_z_e] [--tt _n_u_m_b_e_r] [--aa _a_l_g_o_r_i_t_h_m]

DDEESSCCRRIIPPTTIIOONN
       zzrraammccttll is used to quickly set up zram device parameters, to reset zram
       devices, and to query the status of used zram devices.

       If no option is given, all non-zero size zram devices are shown.

       Note that _z_r_a_m_d_e_v node specified on command line has to already exist.
       The command zzrraammccttll creates a new _/_d_e_v_/_z_r_a_m_<_N_> nodes only when ----ffiinndd
       option specified. It’s possible (and common) that after system boot
       _/_d_e_v_/_z_r_a_m_<_N_> nodes are not created yet.

OOPPTTIIOONNSS
       --aa, ----aallggoorriitthhmm llzzoo|llzz44|llzz44hhcc|ddeeffllaattee|884422|zzssttdd
           Set the compression algorithm to be used for compressing data in
           the zram device.

       --ff, ----ffiinndd
           Find the first unused zram device. If a ----ssiizzee argument is present,
           then initialize the device.

       --nn, ----nnoohheeaaddiinnggss
           Do not print a header line in status output.

       --oo, ----oouuttppuutt _l_i_s_t
           Define the status output columns to be used. If no output
           arrangement is specified, then a default set is used. Use ----hheellpp to
           get a list of all supported columns.

       ----oouuttppuutt--aallll
           Output all available columns.

       ----rraaww
           Use the raw format for status output.

       --rr, ----rreesseett
           Reset the options of the specified zram device(s). Zram device
           settings can be changed only after a reset.

       --ss, ----ssiizzee _s_i_z_e
           Create a zram device of the specified _s_i_z_e. Zram devices are
           aligned to memory pages; when the requested _s_i_z_e is not a multiple
           of the page size, it will be rounded up to the next multiple. When
           not otherwise specified, the unit of the _s_i_z_e parameter is bytes.

           The _s_i_z_e argument may be followed by the multiplicative suffixes
           KiB (=1024), MiB (=1024*1024), and so on for GiB, TiB, PiB, EiB,
           ZiB and YiB (the "iB" is optional, e.g., "K" has the same meaning
           as "KiB") or the suffixes KB (=1000), MB (=1000*1000), and so on
           for GB, TB, PB, EB, ZB and YB.

       --tt, ----ssttrreeaammss _n_u_m_b_e_r
           Set the maximum number of compression streams that can be used for
           the device. The default is use all CPUs and one stream for kernels
           older than 4.6.

       --hh, ----hheellpp
           Display help text and exit.

       --VV, ----vveerrssiioonn
           Print version and exit.

EEXXIITT SSTTAATTUUSS
       zzrraammccttll returns 0 on success, nonzero on failure.

FFIILLEESS
       _/_d_e_v_/_z_r_a_m_[_0_._._N_]
           zram block devices

EEXXAAMMPPLLEE
       The following commands set up a zram device with a size of one gigabyte
       and use it as swap device.

            # zramctl --find --size 1024M
            /dev/zram0
            # mkswap /dev/zram0
            # swapon /dev/zram0
            ...
            # swapoff /dev/zram0
            # zramctl --reset /dev/zram0

